  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nA fairly well-developed low pressure system will be imminently \nmoving across the area through tonight, its west-to-east warm \nfront bisecting MN/WI and its trailing cold front sagging across\nSD into WY. Warm air advection, along with moisture advection, \nis occurring along and south of the front, now evident with wind\nshifts to the SE and S over far southern MN and dewpoints \ncreeping into the lower 70s. North of the front, temperatures \nremain in the 80s with dewpoints in the upper 60s to near 70. \nThis front may wobble slightly north/south thru this evening but\ngenerally slide eastward as the low moves into west-central MN \nlater this evening then pushes east to the TC Metro by around \nsunrise Monday morning. If any precip occurs for the rest of \ntoday thru overnight tonight, it'll be very light and north of \nI-94. The cold front will make enough progression eastward \ntonight into tomorrow such that much of the humidity will be \nswept eastward, but still hanging on in far eastern MN \n(including the TC metro) into western WI tomorrow. So while much\nof the WFO MPX coverage area, especially those that escaped \ncloud cover, experienced heat index values into the low-mid 90s \ntoday, most locales in MN will have peak index values under 90 \non Monday while western WI will have peak heat index values in \nthe 90s.\n\nThe lower dewpoints spreading eastward across MN and WI will\nremain in place through most of the week. Thus, despite \ntemperatures ranging through the 80s all week, dewpoints will \ngenerally remain from the upper 50s to upper 60s, making heat \nindex values no more than 1-2 degrees over actual temperatures \nfrom Tuesday onward for the entirety of the WFO MPX coverage \narea.\n\nAs for precipitation, high pressure will fill in behind the\ncurrent progressive frontal system, making for dry weather for\ntonight through Tuesday. The next system ejects out of the \nPlains late Tuesday night into Wednesday morning, making early \nWednesday morning the best chance for fairly widespread \nprecipitation across the WFO MPX coverage area. A few \nthunderstorms are possible but with the frontal boundary of the \nsystem remaining to the south of MN/WI, strong-severe \nthunderstorms are not expected. The boundary will generally \nremain stalled to the south of our coverage area, keeping any \nappreciable precipitation away but a few weak shortwaves mid-to-\nlate week may squeeze out a few hundredths here and there.",
